t; a real 429 body was never captured), whether an invalid key arrives as 400 `API_KEY_INVALID`, a GitHub Actions run on this change, and behaviour under real ingest load. Spike code was copied, not imported, and `spikes/` is untouched.

- 2026-11-02: Task 1.4 (chunker) closed; recorded together with Task 1.5, so the date is when it was written down. `lib/chunking/` exports `normalizePageText`, `PIPELINE_VERSION`, `chunkPages(pages, options)` and `paginateText(text, options)`, and the `PageInput` (`{ page, text }`), `Chunk` and option types. Normalization lives in one place (`normalize.ts`) and `PIPELINE_VERSION = 1` matches the `documents.pipeline_version` default; changing any normalization rule changes the stored offsets, so the version must be bumped and old documents re-ingested. `normalizePageText` converts CR and CRLF to LF, tabs and NBSP to spaces, collapses space runs and trailing spaces, collapses 3 or more newlines to 2, trims the page, applies NFKC, removes soft hyphens, zero-width and bidi marks, the BOM and control characters (including NUL), keeps ZWJ, ZWNJ and variation selectors, replaces unpaired surrogates with U+FFFD, and is idempotent. `chunkPages` normalizes each page, then returns `Chunk { chunkIndex, pageNumber, charStart, charEnd, content }` where `content === normalizePageText(page.text).slice(charStart, charEnd)` (offsets are UTF-16 code units into the normalized page text, never the raw text). Chunks never cross a page and nothing overlaps across pages; pages keep input order; empty or whitespace-only pages are skipped and `chunkIndex` stays contiguous. Paragraphs are grouped greedily up to `maxChars`; an oversized paragraph falls back to a sentence, then a line, then whitespace boundary, then a hard cut that never splits a surrogate pair. Overlap snaps to a paragraph, sentence or word start and is usually a little smaller than requested, so read it from the offsets; a short final piece is merged when it fits and is never dropped. Defaults are `maxChars` 1200 and `overlapChars` 150 (provisional starting values, not empirically validated; the Slice 2 eval is where they get tuned), with a hard ceiling of 4000 for `chunks.content`. Sizes are in characters, not tokens. Invalid options, duplicate or invalid page numbers throw `RangeError`, and non-string page text throws `TypeError`. `paginateText` cuts `.txt` and `.md` text into virtual pages of about 4000 characters (numbered from 1, normalized first, ending at paragraph boundaries where possible, with no text lost or duplicated); Markdown is not parsed. The 30-page limit is deliberately not enforced in the chunker: extraction (Task 1.5) and ingestion own it. The module has no `server-only` marker on purpose, because the citation viewer needs the same normalization on the client to match chunk text against a rendered PDF text layer. Tests: `tests/unit/chunking-chunk-pages.test.ts`, `chunking-normalize.test.ts` and `chunking-paginate.test.ts` (82 test blocks) cover every Task 1.4 acceptance item plus overlap snapping, Unicode and CJK handling, validation and a generated-input invariants test. Verified: marked done by the project owner; in Claude's sandbox (dependencies installed with npm, fixtures regenerated) the full unit suite passed except `sample-docs.test.ts`, whose seed PDF was missing from the repository snapshot, and lint and typecheck passed for the whole repository. Not tested: chunk counts and chunk quality on the real sample documents (known only once Task 1.6 ingests them, which closes the open note in the 2026-11-01 Task 0.9 entry), retrieval quality, and whether 1200 and 150 are good settings.
- 2026-11-02: Task 1.5 (extraction module) closed. `lib/pdf/` exports `extractPages(buffer: Uint8Array, mimeType: string): Promise<PageInput[]>`, returning the `{ page, text }` shape from `lib/chunking`. PDFs use `unpdf` 1.9.1 as validated in Task 0.6 (spike code was reimplemented, not imported; `spikes/` is untouched): one entry per physical page, 1-based, blank pages kept, text not normalized (the chunker normalizes). `text/plain` and `text/markdown` are decoded as strict UTF-8 and passed to the existing `paginateText()`; Markdown is not parsed or stripped. Checks run in this order: argument types (wrong types throw `TypeError`), size over 5 MiB, zero bytes, MIME type (lowercased, parameters ignored; only `application/pdf`, `text/plain` and `text/markdown`), then content: `%PDF-` within the first 1024 bytes for PDFs (the spike's rule), and for text no NUL bytes (this also rejects UTF-16) and valid UTF-8 (a BOM is stripped; invalid bytes are rejected, never replaced). The MIME type is a routing hint only. Limits (`lib/pdf/limits.ts`, kept local until `lib/config.ts` exists): 30 pages and 5 MiB. The 30-page limit also applies to virtual text pages, because `documents.page_count` is capped at 30 for every type; it is checked before any PDF text is extracted. Errors: every file problem is an `ExtractionError` subclass whose `code` equals a `documents.error_reason` value (`ScannedPdfError` `scanned_pdf`, `TooManyPagesError` `too_many_pages`, `FileTooLargeError` `file_too_large`, `UnsupportedMimeTypeError` and `InvalidFileSignatureError` `unsupported_type`, `CorruptPdfError` `corrupt_file`, `EncryptedPdfError` `encrypted_file`, `EmptyDocumentError` `empty_document`, `UnexpectedExtractionError` `internal_error`). Messages are fixed strings and no `cause` is kept, so raw library output never reaches callers. Only `PasswordException` (encrypted) and `InvalidPDFException`, `MissingPDFException` and `FormatError` (corrupt) get specific codes; any other library error becomes `internal_error`. Whitespace-only text is `empty_document`; a PDF with no text is treated as scanned. Owner-password-only PDFs are accepted (the product decision Task 0.6 left open); only PDFs that need a password are rejected. Cleanup: the document proxy in unpdf 1.9.1 has no `destroy()`, so `loadingTask.destroy()` runs in a `finally` and a cleanup failure is swallowed, never replacing the result or the original error. Scanned detection is a provisional heuristic, not OCR detection: a PDF whose average non-whitespace characters per page is below 25 (`MIN_AVG_CHARS_PER_PAGE`) throws `ScannedPdfError`. The 25 comes only from synthetic fixtures (two generated scans averaged 0 and 5; the sparsest real-text fixture 59) and was never checked on a real scan. Known limits: a real scan with a stamp, Bates number or OCR layer averaging 25 or more passes and yields junk chunks; an image-heavy text PDF such as slides can be falsely rejected; it uses the average, so a half-scanned document can pass; garbled text from unmapped fonts counts as characters and passes. Re-check on real documents before relying on it. Tests: `tests/unit/pdf-extract.test.ts` (45 tests) uses fixtures copied from `spikes/pdf/fixtures/` into `tests/fixtures/pdf/` (so they survive the Task 6.9 removal of `/spikes`) and `tests/unit/helpers/pdf-builder.ts` to build the 31-page and blank-page PDFs in memory; the cleanup tests wrap the real PDF.js objects with spies. Verified: lint, typecheck and unit tests pass locally, as reported by the project owner. Claude's own sandbox checks (fixtures regenerated with the spike's `make_fixtures.py`, dependencies installed with npm): the new tests fail when cleanup, the threshold or the page limit are deliberately broken, and the module ran with the real `server-only` marker through `tsx --conditions=react-server`. Not verified: `next build` (nothing in `app/` imports `lib/pdf` yet), behaviour on real scanned or real-world PDFs, and a GitHub Actions run on this change. Not built: OCR, size or page limits for anything but the values above, and any ingestion, storage or API use (Task 1.6 and Slice 4).
- 2026-11-03: Task 1.6 (ingestion service and sample seeding script) closed. `features/ingestion/` exports `ingestDocument(documentId, { store, embedder, options })`, which returns an outcome (`ready`, `failed` with a `documents.error_reason`, `not_claimed`, `not_found`, `claim_lost`) instead of throwing for expected results; it throws only for a non-UUID id or invalid options (before touching anything) and when a failure cannot be recorded (the document then stays `processing` until it goes stale). The signature differs from BUILD_PLAN's `ingestDocument(documentId)` because the Supabase admin client does not exist until Task 1.7, so persistence is an injected `IngestStore` (`createSupabaseIngestStore(client)` is the Supabase implementation, taking the service-role client) and the embedder is an injected `EmbeddingProvider`. Flow: load the document, `claim_document_for_ingest`, download, `extractPages`, `chunkPages` (no chunking options changed), embed in batches of 10 with a 10 s pause between batches, upsert each batch into `chunks` on `(document_id, chunk_index)` (vectors sent in pgvector text format), then `finalize_ingestion` with the embedder's model and `PIPELINE_VERSION`. Offsets are stored exactly as `chunkPages` produced them. Any failure after the claim calls `fail_ingestion` (which deletes partial chunks): `ExtractionError` stores its `code`, `RateLimitError` and `ProviderUnavailableError` that survive the retries store `provider_unavailable`, and everything else (including `BadRequestError` and a bad API key, which are never retried) stores `internal_error`. If `finalize_ingestion` reports `document_not_processing` the run returns `claim_lost` and does not call `fail_ingestion`, so it cannot delete the chunks of whoever re-claimed the document. Retry policy (provisional, from the Task 0.5 spike): on `RateLimitError` or `ProviderUnavailableError` that the adapter's own short retries did not clear, wait 15 s then 30 s and try again (two retries); a provid