verification. `types/ask-stream.ts` (types only) defines `AskStreamEvent`, `StreamSource` and `StreamErrorCode`; `lib/ask-stream/` holds `events.ts` (`encodeStreamEvent`, `parseStreamEvent`, `isTerminalStreamEvent`), `parse.ts` (`readAskStream`), `errors.ts` and `index.ts`. None of it imports `server-only`, because the server encodes and the browser parses. Task 1.9 needs only `encodeStreamEvent`. Wire format: each event is one SSE frame, `data: {"type": "...", ...}\n\n`, with the event type inside the JSON and no `event:` field. A `: comment` frame is allowed (for example an opening flush) and is skipped. Events: `sources` (`sources: { marker, documentId, documentTitle, page, snippet }[]`), `token` (`text`), `done` (`citations: number[]`, `invalidCitations: number[]`), `not_found` (optional `closest?: StreamSource[]`, omitted rather than null when absent), `limit` (`scope: "user" | "global"`) and `error` (`code: StreamErrorCode`, safe `message`). `marker` and `page` are positive integers; the arrays in `done` hold integers (an invalid marker such as 0 or 9 belongs in `invalidCitations`). Slice 3 may add `charStart` and `charEnd` to `StreamSource` additively: the parser ignores unknown fields, and rebuilds each source from its known fields only. `StreamErrorCode` is `search_unavailable`, `llm_unavailable`, `interrupted` or `internal_error`. The plan fixed only that `error` has a code and a safe message, so these four are a provisional choice taken from the ARCHITECTURE failure-mode table. The parser rejects an unknown code as a protocol error (client and server deploy together), so a new code means editing the type and the `ERROR_CODES` table, which the compiler checks for completeness; the client chooses the user-facing text and `message` is only a fallback. Terminal events: `done`, `not_found`, `limit` and `error` each end a complete stream. `readAskStream(body)` yields events as bytes arrive, then stops and cancels the reader right after the first terminal event; later bytes are never parsed, even if malformed. Failures: a stream that closes without a terminal event throws `StreamIncompleteError`; a final frame that never received its blank line is discarded (as the SSE specification says) and so also counts as incomplete. Invalid JSON, a payload that is not an object with a string `type`, or a known event type with an invalid shape throws `StreamProtocolError` (the message names the event and field and never echoes the payload). A failed read throws `StreamInterruptedError` with the original error as `cause`; an `AbortError` is rethrown unchanged. All three extend `AskStreamError`. Unknown event types: `parseStreamEvent` returns `null` and the stream reader skips them, so an old client survives a new server event type. A stream consisting only of unknown events is still incomplete. Framing handled: events split across chunks, several events in one chunk, CRLF (including a CR and LF in different chunks), multi-byte characters split across chunks (`TextDecoder` with `stream: true`), comment lines and frames, extra blank lines, non-data fields (`event:`, `id:`, `retry:`, ignored) and multiple `data:` lines in one frame (joined with a newline). A lone CR as a line ending is not supported. The errors live in `errors.ts`, not `parse.ts`, to avoid a circular import with `events.ts`. Tests: `tests/unit/ask-stream-events.test.ts` and `tests/unit/ask-stream-parse.test.ts` cover the four BUILD_PLAN acceptance cases plus the edge cases above and a check that no module in the folder imports `server-only`. Verified locally (as reported by the project owner): the full Vitest suite passes, including both new test files;yarn lint and yarn typecheck are clean. Not decided or tested: `maxDuration` and the `/api/ask` route (Task 1.9), the UI and its error states (Task 1.10), behaviour against a real LLM stream or a real Vercel deployment, other browsers, and any limit on how large an unfinished frame may grow in memory (the parser does not cap the buffer).
